   "old": "<img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/trna_loading-1.svg\" alt=\"Loading a tRNA in two steps, both catalyzed by aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase. Step 1: an amino acid (carboxylate, alpha carbon with NH3+ and H, side chain R) reacts with ATP; pyrophosphate is released and AMP is attached to the carboxyl group, giving aminoacyl-AMP. Step 2: the amino acid is transferred to the 3' OH of its tRNA as an ester, giving aminoacyl-tRNA (a charged tRNA), and AMP is released. Each amino acid has its own synthetase that matches it to the right tRNA.\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:600px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" /><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/trna_loading-2.svg\" alt=\"\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:600px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" />",
   "new": "<img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/trna_loading-v2-1.svg\" alt=\"Loading a tRNA in two steps, both catalyzed by aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase. Step 1: an amino acid (carboxylate, alpha carbon with NH3+ and H, side chain R) reacts with ATP; pyrophosphate is released and AMP is attached to the carboxyl group, giving aminoacyl-AMP. Step 2: the amino acid is transferred to the 3' OH of its tRNA as an ester, giving aminoacyl-tRNA (a charged tRNA), and AMP is released. Each amino acid has its own synthetase that matches it to the right tRNA.\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:600px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" /><img src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/trna_loading-v2-2.svg\" alt=\"\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;width:600px;max-width:100%;height:auto\" />",
