 "597197": [
  {
   "old": "<img alt=\"Glycolysis and gluconeogenesis side by side. The shared intermediates run down the center: glucose, glucose 6-phosphate, fructose 6-phosphate, fructose 1,6-bisphosphate, the triose phosphates, 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate through 2-phosphoglycerate, phosphoenolpyruvate, and pyruvate. On the left, glycolysis runs downward; its three irreversible steps are hexokinase using ATP, phosphofructokinase-1 using ATP, and pyruvate kinase making ATP. On the right, gluconeogenesis runs upward using four bypass enzymes: pyruvate carboxylase in the mitochondrion uses ATP and biotin to make oxaloacetate, PEP carboxykinase uses GTP to make phosphoenolpyruvate, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase hydrolyzes a phosphate to give fructose 6-phosphate, and glucose-6-phosphatase in the liver hydrolyzes glucose 6-phosphate to free glucose. The seven reversible steps are shared by both pathways. Lactate, alanine, and glycerol enter on the right as gluconeogenic precursors.\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597197-4-1.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597197-4-2.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"760\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597197-4-3.svg\" />",
   "new": "<img alt=\"Glycolysis and gluconeogenesis side by side. The shared intermediates run down the center: glucose, glucose 6-phosphate, fructose 6-phosphate, fructose 1,6-bisphosphate, the triose phosphates, 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate through 2-phosphoglycerate, phosphoenolpyruvate, and pyruvate. On the left, glycolysis runs downward; its three irreversible steps are hexokinase using ATP, phosphofructokinase-1 using ATP, and pyruvate kinase making ATP. On the right, gluconeogenesis runs upward using four bypass enzymes: pyruvate carboxylase in the mitochondrion uses ATP and biotin to make oxaloacetate, PEP carboxykinase uses GTP to make phosphoenolpyruvate, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase hydrolyzes a phosphate to give fructose 6-phosphate, and glucose-6-phosphatase in the liver hydrolyzes glucose 6-phosphate to free glucose. The seven reversible steps are shared by both pathways. Lactate, alanine, and glycerol enter on the right as gluconeogenic precursors.\" width=\"660\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597197-4-v2-1.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"660\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597197-4-v2-2.svg\" /><img alt=\"\" width=\"660\" style=\"display:block;margin:0;max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"https://itsdrdan.github.io/textbook/figures/print/fig-p597197-4-v2-3.svg\" />",
   "count": 1,
